<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Checking in on Health</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Using Health Technology to Form Good Habits</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Analyze opinion editorials on general interest topics. Reading: Evaluate informational content from various sources.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can analyze opinion editorials on health and wellness topics, identifying the writer's stance and supporting arguments (e.g., "The author believes that regular exercise improves mental health because it reduces stress and boosts energy.")
Can evaluate informational content from articles about using healthy technology to develop good habits, identifying key points and potential biases (e.g., "The article promotes wearable fitness trackers as essential for improving exercise routines but overlooks privacy concerns related to data collection.")
Can interpret data from health-related infographics, such as fitness plans or mental health statistics (e.g., "This chart shows that 70% of students experience stress during exam season, suggesting the need for better mental health resources.")</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a series of images showing different health technologies (e.g., fitness trackers, meditation apps, smart water bottles, sleep monitors). Students work in pairs to quickly list 2-3 benefits and 1-2 drawbacks of each technology. The teacher prompts with questions like, "How do these help us maintain our well-being?" or "What habits can these apps support?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary from the previous lesson, focusing on the target sounds: /b/ in 'balance,' 'boost,' 'habits' contrasting with /p/ in 'practice,' 'sleep pattern.' Also, /s/ in 'stress,' 'sleep' contrasting with /z/ in 'exercise,' 'energized.' The teacher clearly enunciates sentences using the present perfect continuous to highlight ongoing health efforts, such as "I have been tracking my hydration levels" or "She has been practicing mindfulness meditation." The teacher then highlights the rhythm of these sentences, emphasizing content words like "tracking" and reducing auxiliary verbs like "have" (/həv/).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> After modeling, the teacher asks:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What's the difference between the 'b' sound in 'boost' and the 'p' sound in 'practice'? Can you give me another word for each?" (Checks phonemic awareness).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'I have been exercising,' does it mean I exercised once, or I'm still doing it? What does it tell us about the habit?" (Checks understanding of present perfect continuous and 'habit').</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students receive a short opinion editorial titled "The Digital Path to Better Health: More Than Just a Trend" or an article about using healthy technology, along with an infographic illustrating health statistics or fitness plans. The texts feature vocabulary related to *health, fitness, well-being, hydration, mindfulness, balance, habits, stress, tracker, app, sleep pattern, activity level, feedback, exercise, drink, sleep, practice, recommend, maintain, improve, track, support, boost, meditate, hydrate, manage* and uses present perfect continuous, second conditional, and modals for possibility. (e.g., "Many individuals *have been tracking* their sleep patterns to *improve* their restorative rest. If you *had* a fitness *tracker*, you *might* feel more motivated to *exercise* regularly.")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpret and analyze informational content from various sources (opinion editorial, article, infographic) on general interest topics (health technology), identifying key points, arguments, and potential biases.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the opinion editorial/article once quickly to get the main idea and answer: "What is the author's overall opinion about using health technology to form good habits?" and "What is the primary message of the infographic?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Statements:</b> Students read a second time and mark 5-7 True/False statements based on specific details from the text and infographic. (e.g., "The article suggests that all health apps are reliable and essential for fitness." or "The infographic indicates a significant increase in stress levels among young adults.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match key arguments or statistics from the text/infographic with their corresponding implications or supporting evidence. (e.g., match a benefit of a fitness *tracker* to a habit it helps *maintain*).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Analyzing opinion editorials and evaluating informational content. Focus on recognizing and applying Present perfect continuous, Second conditional, and Modals for possibility, alongside target vocabulary: *health, fitness, well-being, hydration, mindfulness, balance, habits, stress, tracker, app, sleep pattern, activity level, feedback, exercise, drink, sleep, practice, recommend, maintain, improve, track, support, boost, meditate, hydrate, manage, should, might, must, could, can, balanced, hydrated, healthy, regular, current, reliable, essential, preventive, mindful, energized, nutritious, consistent, flexible, restorative, calm, goal-setting*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary & Grammar Cloze:</b> Students receive a modified version of the opinion editorial with blanks where key vocabulary (e.g., *mindfulness, hydration, tracker, boost, regulate*) and grammatical structures (e.g., *has been practicing, if you had, might need*) have been removed. They fill in the blanks using a word bank or by recalling from the original text. The teacher emphasizes sentence rhythm when reading aloud examples with present perfect continuous.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Argument Analysis Matching:</b> Students are given a list of sentences (A-E) that are either main arguments from the opinion editorial, supporting details, or counterarguments. They must match these sentences with different sections or paragraphs of the original text (1-5) and identify the author's stance. This activity focuses on evaluating informational content and identifying arguments. For example: "If everyone *exercised* regularly, stress levels *might* significantly decrease." (Second conditional, Modals for possibility)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Conditional & Modal Application:</b> In pairs, students use the information from the article and infographic to complete sentences: "If I *had* more free time, I *would use* a health *app* to ______." or "Someone experiencing high *stress* levels *might* find that ______ *could* be a helpful *practice*." They then compare their answers, checking for logical connections and appropriate use of the target grammar and vocabulary. They are encouraged to use formal expressions when discussing these health suggestions.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Infographic Analysis and Recommendation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in groups of three. Each group receives a different health-related infographic (e.g., "Benefits of Consistent Sleep," "Impact of Hydration on Energy," "Mental Health Statistics for Teens"). They must:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Analyze:</b> Interpret the data presented in their infographic, identifying key points and trends. (e.g., "This chart shows that 70% of students experience *stress* during exam season, suggesting the need for better *mental health* resources.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Evaluate:</b> Discuss how health technology could address the issues or enhance the positive trends shown in their infographic. They should reference the opinion editorial/article read earlier. (e.g., "If students *used* a *mindfulness app*, they *might* learn to *manage* their *stress* better.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Recommend:</b> Prepare a short verbal summary (3-4 sentences) for the class, recommending specific health technologies or habits based on their infographic analysis. They should use the present perfect continuous to talk about ongoing efforts, the second conditional for hypothetical improvements, and modals for possibility when giving suggestions. They must use polite and formal expressions (e.g., "Could you recommend a reliable *app* for *sleep pattern* monitoring?"). One student from each group presents their summary to the class.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher circulates, providing support and noting language use, especially the correct application of grammar and vocabulary in context, and pragmatic functions of suggesting and showing empathy. Students are encouraged to express empathy if their infographic deals with challenges (e.g., "That sounds tough. I hope you're resting.").</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist (focusing on reading comprehension and application of target language during group work and presentation). Peer Assessment Checklist (students evaluate their peers' presentations based on criteria).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group's verbal summary and recommendations from Stage 4 will be assessed. Additionally, students complete a short written task where they analyze a new, short paragraph about a health *trend* or *app*, identifying the author's stance and one supporting argument, and offering one polite suggestion for improving *well-being* using a modal verb. (e.g., "The author believes that daily *meditation* *boosts* *energy* because it reduces *stress*. You *might want to try* a short *mindfulness* *app* if you are feeling overwhelmed.")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Reading Comprehension & Analysis:</b> Accuracy in identifying key points, arguments, and interpreting data from the infographic/paragraph.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Appropriate and accurate use of words like *health, fitness, well-being, hydration, mindfulness, balance, habits, stress, tracker, app, improve, maintain, boost, manage, balanced, reliable, essential, mindful* (e.g., at least 5 relevant words).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar Application:</b> Correct application of present perfect continuous, second conditional, and modals for possibility.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic & Sociolinguistic Competence:</b> Ability to give polite suggestions (e.g., "You *might* consider...") and express empathy when appropriate. Use of formal expressions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Clear articulation, especially of /b/ vs /p/, /s/ vs /z/, and sentence rhythm with present perfect continuous, ensuring the message is easily understood.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following prompts on a small slip of paper:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of analyzing the opinion editorial or infographic today, and why?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel now in identifying the main arguments in a health article and using modals to suggest health tips?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did today's reading activities help me better understand the influence of health technology on forming good habits?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new thing I learned about reading critically or about health technology that I can apply to my own life?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What reading comprehension strategies worked best for 12th graders when analyzing opinion editorials and infographics? Were students able to effectively identify bias and author's stance? Did the integration of grammar (present perfect continuous, second conditional, modals) and vocabulary feel natural within the reading activities? What adjustments are needed for Lesson 3 to ensure a smooth transition from reading analysis to active speaking and interaction, specifically using the learned content to discuss health check-ins and technology?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 3, students will engage in speaking activities and interactions based on the health technology topics and critical reading skills developed today. They will practice giving health suggestions and expressing empathy in a simulated "Checking in on Health" scenario, applying the vocabulary and grammar to express their opinions and recommendations verbally.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>